# Every path, deliberately.
#
# Enumerating paths invited a regress with no natural stopping point: owning
# the workflow left the scripts it calls unowned; owning the scripts left the
# package manifests that name them unowned; owning the manifests left the build
# and test files those scripts dispatch unowned. Each step, a pull request
# could still have turned a required check into a no-op without touching an
# owned path.
#
# The repository guard runs from the proposed pull-request tree, so the tree
# that can weaken a check is the tree under review. This repository holds one
# product with one owner, so there is no path here that someone else legitimately
# owns and the honest boundary is all of it.
#
# See docs/operations/github-setup.md: with a single owner, required Code Owner
# review cannot be satisfied on that owner's own pull requests, so the manual
# completion contract applies until there is a second reviewer.
